{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"PRE-IMPLEMENTATION RESEARCH against the landed code. FOUR findings contradict this plan as written; recording before the story is claimed.\n\n1. \"No new gate logic is written; wiring only\" IS NOT TRUE. parity_report() is pure and needs no change — it takes two index-paired sequences of ItemRecord and returns ParityReport(passed, gating_failures, metrics). But it has ZERO production callers (grep of src/ finds only container_fidelity_report, prerequisite_fidelity_report and parallel_run_and_diff), it is NOT exported from rebar.llm, there is no CLI command, and there is NO serializer — so \"record the ParityReport\" has no existing artifact path. More importantly there is NO ItemRecord adapter: run_eval returns {prompt, epochs, gate, passed, coverage, epoch_pass, junit} — pass/fail per EPOCH, not per-item decision+label. Building that mapping is NET-NEW work and it is load-bearing, because criterion (b) \"zero decision-level flips\" is scored on it.\n\n2. GOLD COVERAGE FORCES POOLING. parity.py sets MIN_GOLD_ITEMS = 20 and FAILS (not silently passes) below it. No single eval spec's gold_set reaches 20 — the largest is plan-review-container at 12. So the driver MUST pool gold items across specs, or explicitly lower min_gold and justify it. Also note the CI cost rail REBAR_EVAL_MAX_CASES=12 used by the existing live job truncates dataset[:12], which would decapitate the two largest gold sources (plan-review-finder 42 cases, plan-review-container 24) — applying that cap here would likely trip the gold-coverage failure. Run uncapped, or cap high enough to preserve gold.\n\n3. THE CORPUS BARELY PRODUCES BLOCKING VERDICTS. Only completion-verifier.eval.yaml scores a coarse PASS/FAIL (6 fail / 5 pass cases). Every other spec is scored on FINDINGS PRESENCE, not a verdict — several say so explicitly (code-review-security: \"scored on FINDINGS presence, not a FAIL/BLOCK verdict\"). This matters because the pilot recorded on this ticket was PASS/PASS, and agreement on easy PASSes is the weakest possible evidence. The decision-mapping choice (expect:finding/fail -> block, expect:pass -> advisory/dropped) is where the real design judgment sits.\n\n4. THE TWO ARMS WOULD DIFFER IN TWO VARIABLES, NOT ONE — this is the sharpest finding and it invalidates a naive run. Flipping the provider is an ENV change (REBAR_LLM_MODEL=bedrock:...), but review_kernel/verify.py:269 returns the verifier default ONLY when model == default_model. Any Bedrock id is a non-default value, so the Pass-2/Pass-4 sonnet downgrade STOPS APPLYING on the Bedrock arm while it still applies on the Anthropic arm. Separately, Pass-1 runs the YAML model_ladder (plan-review.yaml:62-65 = haiku/sonnet/opus as BARE names), and bare \"claude-*\" infers provider anthropic (config.py:333-341) — so a \"Bedrock\" run may still send its Pass-1 finders to DIRECT ANTHROPIC. Both effects mean provider is NOT the only variable, so any criterion failure is unattributable and any pass is unrepresentative of production. The driver must pin per-pass models on BOTH arms explicitly, and the run must assert ZERO calls to a non-Bedrock provider on the v2 arm (the REBAR_USAGE_LOG rig already records per-call model).\n\nCOST SHAPE, so the job is sized before it is funded: ~534 model calls per arm per epoch across the 21 dataset-bearing specs; at N=3 both arms that is ~3,200 calls. At concurrency 1 (eval.py runs sequentially) that is roughly 9 hours wall clock, which EXCEEDS the 6-hour GitHub-hosted job limit — so the job needs sharding per spec (matrix) or splitting. Model the job on .github/workflows/prompt-eval.yml's eval-live (workflow_dispatch/schedule gated, continue-on-error, REBAR_USAGE_LOG summarized into the job summary), which is the existing env-gated live-LLM pattern.\n\nSEQUENCING CONSEQUENCE: this story cannot produce a valid ParityReport until the per-pass model mapping is expressible, which is the same gap recorded on eb6e and blocked by 1903 (temperature override) and a574 (region). Running the corpus before that would spend the full ~3,200 calls on a comparison that cannot be attributed."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785459942377200001,"uuid":"cf930af5-ec32-4525-ba4a-fcdb6ebec4aa"}